    "                              \n\n🌆 spinners                                                 \n\n\n\nRich can render a \u{1b}[94mtree\u{1b}[0m                                      \n(\u{1b}[4;34mhttps://rich.readthedocs.io/en/latest/tree.html\u{1b}[0m) with guide\nlines. A tree is ideal for displaying a file structure, or  \nany other hierarchical data.                                \n\nThe labels of the tree can be simple text or anything else  \nRich can render. Run the following for a demonstration:     \n\n\u{1b}[48;2;39;40;34m                                                            \u{1b}[0m\n\u{1b}[48;2;39;40;34m \u{1b}[0m\u{1b}[38;2;248;248;242;48;2;39;40;34mpython -m rich.tree\u{1b}[0m\u{1b}[48;2;39;40;34m                                       \u{1b}[0m\u{1b}[48;2;39;40;34m \u{1b}[0m\n\u{1b}[48;2;39;40;34m                                                            \u{1b}[0m\n\nThis generates the following output:                        \n\n🌆 markdown                                                 \n\nSee the \u{1b}[94mtree.py\u{1b}[0m                                             \n(\u{1b}[4;34mhttps://github.com/textualize/rich/blob/main/examples/tree.\u{1b}[0m\n\u{1b}[4;34mpy\u{1b}[0m) example for a script that displays a tree view of any   \ndirectory, similar to the linux \u{1b}[1;36;40mtree\u{1b}[0m command.               \n\n\n\nRich can render content in neat \u{1b}[94mcolumns\u{1b}[0m                     \n(\u{1b}[4;34mhttps://rich.readthedocs.io/en/latest/columns.html\u{1b}[0m) with   \nequal or optimal width. Here's a very basic clone of the    \n(MacOS / Linux) \u{1b}[1;36;40mls\u{1b}[0m command which displays a directory       \nlis" +
